   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">hi,<div><br></div><div>is it possible to constrain a unidimensional cfa model in lavaan so that it becomes a simple sum score model? i thought something like this should work:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lavaan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mirt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Science</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model_1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#080\">x =~ 1*Comfort + 1*Work + 1*Future + 1*Benefit</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#080\">  Comfort | 1*t1 + 2*t2 + 3*t3</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#080\">  Work | 1*t1 + 2*t2 + 3*t3</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#080\">  Future | 1*t1 + 2*t2 + 3*t3</span></div><div><span style=\"color:#080\">  Benefit | 1*t1 + 2*t2 + 3*t3&quot;</span></div></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div><div style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\"><span style=\"color:#000\">cfa</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model_1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Science</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> std</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lv </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> std</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ov </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ordered </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> names</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Science</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span></div></div></code></div><br>but i am obviously missing something as predict(model_1) != apply(Science, 1, sum).</div><div><br></div><div>Best, Felix</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "98udUH4KFgAJ",
   "from": "Edward Rigdon",
   "author": "99a06696c483481a",
   "date": "2019-08-07T17:41:56Z",
   "subject": "Re: sum score (ctt) model in lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">No, because factor models are indeterminate. A common factor is not a sum of its indicators, but rather a weighted sum of the mode&#39;s observed variables plus an arbitrary component. Moreover, the &quot;1&quot; loadings in your syntax do not mean that the indicators load perfectly on the common factor. If you use the &quot;lavaan()&quot; function, you will be forced to see more of the machinery behind the factor model.<div><br></div><div>If you want to create a sum, do it outside of lavaan.</div><div><br></div><div>--Ed Rigdon</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Aug 7, 2019 at 1:23 PM Felix Fischer &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">bea...@gmx.de</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
